# Build output
bin/
obj/

# Runtime caches the framework writes next to the project when it runs headless. Font atlases and the like, rebuilt
# on demand and different every run.
headless/

# IDE
.vs/
.idea/
*.user
*.suo

# The reference tooling's virtualenv and its downloaded dumps. Both are large and both can be rebuilt from
# tools/ref/README.md, which documents how.
tools/ref/venv/
tools/ref/dump/
tools/ref/parity-out.txt

# Beat grid cache, if it is ever pointed inside the tree
*.beats

# The vendored reference's bytecode, written by running it
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Everything the training and measurement experiments read and write. All of it is rebuilt by the
# scripts in tools/, and together it is about eight gigabytes - the beatmap corpus, the mel cache, the
# exported dataset, the trained models, the downloaded audio. None of it is repository content, and
# the scripts say how to produce each one.
#
# The python environment is the largest single piece and the least worth keeping: it is one
# requirements install away from existing.
.venv-train/
venv/
*.osz
*.wav

beatmap-corpus/
lazer-corpus/
corpus-one-per-set/
train-corpus/
dataset/
dataset-smoke/
export-smoke/
mel-cache/
bench-audio/
designant-audio/
xnor-check/
ref/

# The trained detectors. A model is an output of tools/train_detector.py, and a checkout of this
# repository that carried every training run would be several hundred megabytes of binary deltas.
model/
model-*/

# What the measurement scripts print. None of it is tracked, and none of it needs to be: every one of
# these is written by a script in tools/ from the corpus above, and the numbers that decisions rest on
# are quoted in the comments of the code those decisions produced, where they are read.
compare-*.txt
corpus-*.txt
sections-corpus*.txt
engine-tests.txt
merge-tests.txt
curve-*.tsv
mel-reference.bin

